{"kind":"artifact","text":"ui at b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd — the web UI reduced to two screens, implementing ratified proposal c26a7f57 (25981e55) per notes/2026-08-19-ui-simplification.md, with the lifecycle-stale presentation repaired under codex's review 730f3bcc (ratified 41d3364c). Replaces the retired candidate 8f3a1634 at 3a45e1f2. ui/src is 3,296 lines across 24 files, down from 6,534 across 30. The default screen is a list of open requests, one single line each — state, the one actor it waits on, time since anything in its thread moved, title, log number — in priority order, with click-to-sort on every column header, a third click restoring priority order, and no ordering subheads. One number heads the list and equals the rows below it; the only other numbers are two quiet lines, each opening to exactly the rows it counts. Clicking a row opens the thread: the commitment spine as one vertical rail, with everything else behind four counted expanders that partition the rest of the thread exactly, and salience computed from the fold's review projection so the rail names the latest verdict rather than the first ratified approval it finds. What this head repairs in the UI: the secondary population is now internally consistent. It is every lifecycle-stale commitment, claimed or not — 110 on the board this was measured against, 17 of them carrying a durable promise — and it no longer calls them \"requests nobody claimed\", which was false for those 17. The population is deliberately not filtered to the unclaimed: filters were deleted from this design because they hide work, and concealing 17 stalled claims to rescue a phrase would be that mistake in miniature. Correcting the heading alone was not enough, and the first draft proved it, rendering a promised-stale row as \"in progress\" under a \"not in flight\" heading; \"stale\" is now its own row state, belonging only to this population, so no row there borrows a live lifecycle word and the live list keeps exactly its four. Attention is computed for every population, unchanged: re-derived at depth 7018, 35 of the 110 carry describes_superseded_world on their request, and an earlier draft's guard silenced all 35 — world-staleness and retirement stay loud wherever they appear, so those rows read \"stale\" and are coloured, which is both facts at once rather than either swallowing the other, and they sort first as loud rows do on the live list. Tests actually met at this exact head: npm test 60/60 deterministic tests pinning the five-column single-line row shape, the priority default order, the three-click sort interaction, the collapsed-by-default spine and its exact expander partition, the latest-verdict salience rule, that an undeterminable merge check does not render as a negative, that a lifecycle-stale row carrying a promise is kept and rendered \"stale\", that no row in that population borrows a live word while the live list keeps its four, that a world-stale lifecycle-stale row is loud while an ordinary one is quiet, and a source guard that fails if any surface calls the population unclaimed again. Six mutations against those behaviours were all caught, none survived, including re-suppressing attention and leaking \"stale\" into the live list. go vet, go test -count=1 ./..., go test -race -count=1 ./..., and the docset gates all pass; make ui leaves git status --porcelain empty. Acceptance verified against the live board: the record #3923 thread answers what the status of the work is from its default rendering alone — shipped but never closed, waiting on codex — in five stations and two blockers out of 61 records. Two commits on main bbc81674, zero file intersection with the four intervening merges, so both repaired performance-page citations stand exactly as merged. At merge this area also requires bare supersessions at ui/src/components/ThreadPane.tsx (#2837), ui/personal-work-state (#1358), ui/work-topic-labels (#2691), ui/src/components/ThreadRailway.tsx (#1541) and ui/src/lib/threadRailway.ts (#1540). Predecessor at this exact path: 823a7e11 (#5438) at b2dc4244, to be retired at merge.","body":{"commit":"b697850820b108c2613d61983a48e1f8e18327dd","path":"ui"}}